<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter6_arrays">
    <title>Arrays</title>

    <p>
        In Chapter 5, we learned that a structure can combine related values
        into one object. For example, a <c>Point</c> structure combines an
        <c>x</c> coordinate and a <c>y</c> coordinate.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_one_point_before_array" language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

Point point{3.0, 4.0};
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        A structure describes one object. What if a program needs to process
        1,000 points? Creating 1,000 separately named variables would be
        inconvenient and difficult to process with a loop. We need a container
        that can store many objects under one name.
    </p>

    <p>
        The standard-library type <c>std::array</c> provides such a container
        when the number of elements is fixed. The structure defines what one
        element contains; the array organizes many elements.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_create_std_array"
             language="cpp"
             interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << scores[index] << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        The type <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;</c> means that <c>scores</c> stores
        five <c>int</c> values. Its size is fixed when the array is created. The
        member function <c>size()</c> reports the number of elements.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays_initialization">
        <title>Initializing Every Element</title>

        <p>
            An empty pair of braces after an array name has an important meaning.
            In <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; scores{};</c>, the braces request
            <term>value-initialization</term>. Because each element is an
            <c>int</c>, every element is initialized to <c>0</c>.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_value_initialize_std_array"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::array<int, 5> scores{};

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << "scores[" << index << "] = "
                  << scores[index] << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The array contains five initialized element objects. Conceptually,
            its values are:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_array_value_initialization">
            <title>Values after <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; scores{}</c></title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Index</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Value</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The braces do not mean that the array is left empty or that its
            storage is ignored. They initialize all five elements before the
            program uses them. For an array of <c>double</c> values, the elements
            would be initialized to <c>0.0</c>. For an array of <c>bool</c>
            values, they would be initialized to <c>false</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
            If an initializer provides only some values, the remaining elements
            are also value-initialized. For example,
            <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; scores{10, 20};</c> produces the values
            <c>10, 20, 0, 0, 0</c>. Writing a local integer array without an
            initializer, such as <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; scores;</c>, does not
            initialize its <c>int</c> elements to useful values. Using <c>{}</c>
            is a safe way to begin with known values.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays_indexing">
        <title>Accessing Elements by Index</title>

        <p>
            Each array element has an <term>index</term>, which identifies its
            position. The first element has index 0, not index 1. Indexed
            iteration is useful when a program needs to work with a position or
            compare neighboring elements.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_index_std_array"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << "Score " << index + 1 << ": "
                  << scores[index] << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Notice the difference between the array index and the displayed
            score number. The index begins at 0, while the human-friendly label
            begins at 1.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays_passing-to-functions">
        <title>Passing Arrays to Functions</title>

        <p>
            An array can be passed to a function just like other values. Because
            <c>std::array</c> includes its element type and size in its type, the
            parameter also shows those details.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_arrays_function_parameters">
            <title>Ways to pass a <c>std::array</c> to a function</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Parameter</cell>
                    <cell>Effect</cell>
                    <cell>Use when</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; values</c></cell>
                    <cell>The function receives a copy.</cell>
                    <cell>The function needs an independent copy.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>const std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;&amp; values</c></cell>
                    <cell>No copy is made, and the function cannot modify the array.</cell>
                    <cell>The function only needs to inspect the array.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;&amp; values</c></cell>
                    <cell>No copy is made, and the function can modify the original array.</cell>
                    <cell>The function must change the caller's array.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            When a function only reads an array, a <c>const</c> reference is
            usually the appropriate choice. It avoids copying the array and
            documents that the function will not change it. When a function must
            update the caller's array, use a non-<c>const</c> reference.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                For a potentially large array, avoid passing by value unless the
                function intentionally needs an independent copy. Prefer a
                <c>const</c> reference for read-only access and a non-
                <c>const</c> reference when the function is supposed to modify
                the caller's array.
            </p>
        </note>

        <program xml:id="program_pass_array_to_functions"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

void print_scores(const std::array<int, 5>& scores) {
    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        std::cout << scores[index] << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}

void add_bonus_points(std::array<int, 5>& scores, int bonus_points) {
    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        scores[index] += bonus_points;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};

    print_scores(scores);
    add_bonus_points(scores, 5);
    print_scores(scores);

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The function <c>print_scores()</c> receives a read-only reference,
            while <c>add_bonus_points()</c> receives a modifiable reference.
            Both functions work with the original array without making a copy.
        </p>

        <p>
            The size is part of the <c>std::array</c> type. Therefore, a
            function expecting <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;</c> cannot receive an
            array of three integers. General-purpose functions that accept many
            different array sizes require additional techniques that we will
            study later.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter6_arrays_search-summarize">
        <title>Searching and Summarizing Values</title>

        <p>
            A loop can inspect every element to search for a value, count values
            that meet a condition, or calculate a total. These tasks combine
            indexed iteration with the counters and accumulators from the
            previous section.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_summarize_scores"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const std::array<int, 5> scores{82, 91, 76, 88, 95};
    int total = 0;
    int high_score_count = 0;

    for (std::size_t index = 0; index < scores.size(); ++index) {
        total += scores[index];

        if (scores[index] >= 90) {
            ++high_score_count;
        }
    }

    std::cout << "Total: " << total << '\n';
    std::cout << "High scores: " << high_score_count << '\n';
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays_index-short-answer">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The first element of a fixed-size array has index
                <fillin width="4" answer="0" mode="number"/>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays_index-organization">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which loop correctly visits every element of
                <c>std::array&lt;int, 4&gt; values</c> by index?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>for (std::size_t index = 0; index &lt; values.size(); ++index)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The index begins at 0 and stops before <c>values.size()</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>for (std::size_t index = 0; index &lt;= values.size(); ++index)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The final valid index is one less than <c>values.size()</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>for (std::size_t index = 1; index &lt; values.size(); ++index)</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This skips the element at index 0.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays_const-reference-parameter">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function only needs to inspect a
                <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;</c>. Which parameter avoids a copy and
                prevents the function from modifying the caller's array?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>const std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;&amp; values</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A const reference avoids copying and provides read-only access.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; values</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This parameter receives a copy of the entire array.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;&amp; values</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This permits modification, which the function does not need.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter6_arrays_modify-reference-parameter">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function must add 5 to every element of the caller's
                <c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;</c>. Which parameter is appropriate?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;&amp; values</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A non-const reference lets the function modify the original array.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>const std::array&lt;int, 5&gt;&amp; values</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Const references cannot be used to modify the elements.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; values</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>This changes only a copy, so the caller's array remains unchanged.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
